= Self-organization — Simple =

'''Self-organization''' happens when many parts interact locally and create an organized larger pattern without one central controller designing every detail.

A flock of birds is a common example: each bird reacts to nearby birds, yet the whole flock can move in an organized way.

Self-organization does not mean the system has no outside influences.

It can still need energy, resources, or environmental conditions.

